WEBVTT

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:07.672
Before we talk about how conversational AI works or where it is used,

00:00:07.672 --> 00:00:10.560
we need to start with something more basic.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.363
We need to understand what makes an interaction a true conversation,

00:00:14.363 --> 00:00:18.280
and why this kind of AI is different from the systems that came before it.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.907
Most of us have interacted with some form of automation,

00:00:22.907 --> 00:00:26.160
but not all automated systems are conversational.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.161
This distinction matters because it shapes how people experience technology

00:00:32.161 --> 00:00:33.120
in real life.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.400
Let us begin with a simple scenario.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.600
Meet Emily.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.080
Emily needs help with a recent bank transaction.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.600
She calls customer support and is greeted by an automated system.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.004
The voice asks her to press one for account details,

00:00:51.004 --> 00:00:55.840
press 2 for card services, and press 3 for recent transactions.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.120
Emily listens carefully, presses a number, and waits.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.320
A new menu begins to play.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.240
She presses another option and waits again.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.137
After a few more steps, she realizes the option she needs is not

00:01:10.137 --> 00:01:11.080
listed at all.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.440
She hangs up, slightly frustrated, without any solution.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.067
Later that day, Emily opens the bank's website and starts

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:21.120
a chat instead.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:26.280
This time she types I need help with a transaction that looks wrong.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.320
The system responds with a follow up question and asks for more details.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.228
The conversation continues step by step until Emily gets the information she

00:01:37.228 --> 00:01:37.680
needs.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.411
Both interactions involved automation, but only one of them felt like a

00:01:43.411 --> 00:01:44.360
conversation.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.120
This difference helps us understand what conversational AI really is.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:55.896
Conversational AI refers to systems that can engage in dialogue with users in an

00:01:55.896 --> 00:01:57.680
organic and flexible way.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:03.183
These interactions can take place through written text, voice conversations,

00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:05.120
or web-based chat interfaces.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.833
Unlike older systems, conversational AI is not limited to rigid

00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:12.960
menus or predefined paths.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.631
It is designed to understand what users are trying to communicate,

00:02:17.631 --> 00:02:20.720
even when the wording is informal or imperfect.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.696
As a result, interactions feel more responsive and

00:02:24.696 --> 00:02:25.520
more human.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.600
Traditional systems, such as IVR or interactive voice response,

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.160
were built around fixed options.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.246
They work well for simple, predictable requests,

00:02:38.246 --> 00:02:43.996
but struggle when users need to explain context or ask questions in unexpected

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:44.360
ways.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:51.000
Once a user steps outside the predefined flow, these systems cannot easily adapt.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.200
Conversational AI allows users to speak or type naturally.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.880
The system interprets intent and responds in a way that keeps the interaction

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:02.880
moving forward.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:09.200
Instead of forcing users to follow rigid steps, the technology adapts to them.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.840
This is what separates basic automation from true conversational experiences.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:24.280
One of the earliest examples was Eliza, created in 1964 by Joseph Weisenbaum.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.505
Eliza simulated conversation by matching patterns in user input,

00:03:29.505 --> 00:03:35.080
creating the illusion of understanding even though it followed simple rules.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.890
Later, Alice, developed by Richard Wallace,

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:44.017
expanded on this approach using structured pattern matching and scripted

00:03:44.017 --> 00:03:44.720
responses.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:50.400
Cleverbot took another step forward by learning from past conversations with

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.938
real users, allowing it to generate more varied and

00:03:53.938 --> 00:03:55.640
less predictable replies.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.943
Modern assistants such as Siri, Google Assistant,

00:04:00.943 --> 00:04:04.520
and Cortana represent a major leap forward.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:11.112
Advances in language processing and computing power now allow these systems

00:04:11.112 --> 00:04:14.883
to understand context, manage longer conversations,

00:04:14.883 --> 00:04:17.640
and respond more accurately over time.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.129
Today, conversational AI plays a real role in

00:04:21.129 --> 00:04:25.840
how customers find information, get support, and interact with businesses.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.570
Understanding how it differs from older systems sets the foundation for

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.400
everything else in this course.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:37.471
In the next section, we will explore how conversational AI

00:04:37.471 --> 00:04:39.160
works behind the scenes.